**Shiva Sharanas**

The term "Shiva Sharanas" or "Sharana" refers to the devotees of Lord Shiva, specifically associated with the 12th-century movement of Vachanakaras. The word "Sharana" is derived from the Sanskrit root meaning "to surrender" and indicates an individual who has committed themselves to the will of the Lord. This concept embodies not only the act of surrendering but also the respect accorded to others within the spiritual framework.

The Vachanakaras, including prominent figures such as Basavanna, Allama Prabhu, Akka Mahadevi, and Madivala Machideva, produced a body of literature known as Vachanas, which extensively explore themes of devotion and the relationship between the individual and the divine. Within this literature, the notion of "Sharana" occupies a central role, often portrayed as a spiritually elevated state. The Vachanas were instrumental in elucidating the philosophical underpinnings of Shiva worship and the communal values that stem from it.

An illustrative example of the esteemed position of a sharana can be found in the following vachana by Basavanna:

“Look you, the sharana's sleep is telling of beads;  
It's Sivaratri when he wakes and sits;  
Wherever he treads is holy ground;  
As Siva doctrine whatever he speaks;  
The body of Kudala Sanga's Sarana  
Is very abode of Siva!”

The Vachanas, which emerged during the 12th century, emphasize the significance of individual experience in the pursuit of spiritual truth, marking an important development within the broader tradition of Shaivism. 

